  "stage_path": "/private/tmp/suiroai-dsc59/artifacts/dsc60-final-evaluation-2026-10-20/business-writing/runs/business-response-a7a0997d2a704d1ab82e9c91fac5272e",
  "status": "rewritten",
  "text": "The existing restocking list suggests 880 units across 112 product-and-store combinations, with no order suggested for another 224. Use it as a starting point for your buying review: first check the 52 warnings about possible shortages before deliveries arrive, including any attached to zero-order items. The stock figures and ordering policies are synthetic examples, so check actual stock, open orders, delivery lead times and your ordering rules before placing purchases.\n\nThe forecasting method showed a modest improvement over a simple recent-sales average. Forecasts made on 30 November 2025 were compared with recorded units for 1–28 December 2025, covering 336 product-and-store combinations over four weeks. Average absolute error was 1.033 units per product, store and week, versus 1.077 for an average based on the previous 13 calendar weeks with known records. That is 4.06% lower forecast error, with lower error in each of the four weeks. This baseline is a comparison method; your exact current buying process is unknown.\n\nThat is a useful forecasting result, but it does not establish purchasing savings, fewer shortages or increased sales. The method also tended to forecast below recorded quantities, more so than the simple average, so review items at risk of running short carefully. Four weeks is a limited test, and earlier project work had already seen overall historical summaries, so this was not a fully independent test of entirely unseen information.\n\nThe records also leave some demand unknown. Missing days were not filled in, and an item absent from a day’s records could mean an unrecorded sale or unavailable stock rather than no demand. The comparison counted positive quantities on processing and returned orders as well as completed and shipped orders. Treat the result as accuracy against those recorded quantities, rather than a complete picture of customer demand.\n\nThe delivered local routine handles the repeated work of reading and cleaning the supplied sales, estimating quantities for each product and store, and calculating suggested orders using supplied stock, incoming receipts and ordering policies. This reduces repeated manual calculation while leaving purchase decisions with you. The existing list has not been recalculated by this comparison, and automatic uploads or scheduled runs are not established.\n\nFor the measured workload—26 weeks, 336 product-and-store combinations and about 17,400 sales rows—the routine took about 0.43 seconds including startup imports and used about 140 MB of peak memory. It ran on a computer with 128 GB of RAM, not an 8 GB machine; timing will vary. Routine runs require no paid AI model service. Computer, electricity or hosting costs still apply, and the AI-assisted build cost is unknown.\n\nAfter reviewing the shortage warnings, work through the positive orders and check zero orders against shelf shortages. As optional future housekeeping, record stockouts, delivery arrivals, returns and order-status changes consistently. Better records may support better future estimates, without guaranteeing improvement.\n",
